course: everyday
lesson: 414
title: Mum's card: game money is real money
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Ollie was playing a building game on the family tablet when a shiny button offered «MEGA COIN CHEST». He pressed it, the tablet went «ding», and the coins rained down. Ollie thought it was pretend money, like the money in a board game. Then his mum looked at her phone and said, in a very quiet voice that was much worse than shouting: «Ollie. Why has a game taken seventy pounds from my bank?» Mum's card had been saved on the tablet, so every «ding» had been real money.
explain: Ollie's mum contacted the shop, explained a child had bought it by mistake, and asked for the money back; then she removed the saved card and turned on a password for every purchase. Ruby learned the same lesson from the other side: she left the tablet with her little brother Rory for five minutes, and Rory, pressing buttons like a happy woodpecker, bought a pack of gems. He is three. He cannot read. He still managed it. Their mum got the money back that time and switched on the purchase lock, and Ruby, who felt responsible because she had left him with it, stopped leaving him alone with a tablet that could buy things.
explain: Not everyone in the cast made mistakes by accident. Priya saw her dad's card on the kitchen table and copied the numbers onto a scrap of paper «just in case». She felt sneaky all evening, threw the paper away the next day without using it, and then — this was the brave part — told her dad. Noah's friend said «just use your mum's card, she won't notice». Noah said no, and later heard that the friend's mum had noticed, very much. Mia's dad showed her a bank statement with page after page of tiny game charges her big brother had made: £1.99, £0.99, £4.99, again and again. Seeing the list made the money feel real.
explain: Some families make it work with clear rules. Leo can buy one thing a month, and his mum types the password herself, so he plans his buy carefully and nobody argues. When Jayden bought something without asking, he told his mum before she found it, and she was much calmer than if she had discovered it herself. Zara's dad asked her to pay back what she had spent from her pocket money, and it took six weeks. Kai, who had spent «only two pounds», asked why his mum was upset. «It's not about the money,» she said, «it's about the asking.»
fact: Coins and gems bought in a game are paid for with real money from a real bank account.
fact: A card saved on a tablet, phone or console can be used with one tap unless a password is required.
fact: Using someone's card without asking is taking their money, even if it is a parent's.
fact: Parents can set a password or lock so that every purchase needs their permission.
fact: Little children can buy things by pressing buttons at random on a device with a saved card.
fact: When a child buys something by mistake, parents can ask the shop for a refund, but the money does not always come back.
fact: Telling a parent about a purchase before they find it usually makes them calmer.
fact: Paying back money you spent without permission is a fair way to make things right.
fact: A clear family rule about buying, such as one purchase a month, prevents most arguments.
fact: A plan that only works if nobody notices is a bad plan.
fact: Bank statements show every purchase, so small game charges are always seen in the end.
exercise: Why did Ollie press the «MEGA COIN CHEST» button? | He thought the coins were pretend money | He wanted to take money from his mum on purpose | His friend dared him to press it | The game said it was free for him
exercise: How did Ollie's mum find out? | She saw a seventy-pound charge from her bank | The game sent her a thank-you card in the post | Ollie's little sister told on him at bedtime that night | The tablet made a loud alarm noise when the coins were bought
exercise: What did Ollie's mum do after? | Asked the shop for a refund and removed the saved card | Threw the tablet in the bin and banned games for a year | Bought more coins to match | Did nothing and forgot about it
exercise: How could Rory, who cannot read, buy gems? | The tablet had a saved card and no purchase lock | Rory had secretly learned his mum's password by watching her | The gems were free for small children | Ruby helped him on purpose
exercise: How did Ruby feel when Rory bought the gems? | Responsible, because she left the tablet with him | Proud of Rory for being so clever with the tablet | Happy because she got the gems | Not bothered, it was not her fault at all
exercise: How did Priya feel after copying her dad's card numbers? | Sneaky and nervous all evening | Proud and clever, like a real spy | Calm, because it was only just in case | Excited to go shopping online with them
exercise: What was the brave thing Priya did? | Told her dad what she had done | Kept the paper hidden for ever in her drawer | Used the numbers only once, for something small | Gave the numbers to a friend to look after
exercise: Why was copying the card numbers wrong, even without using them? | They belong to her dad, and using them would be taking his money | Card numbers stop working as soon as anyone copies them down on paper | It would break the card | It is fine as long as it stays on paper
exercise: Noah's friend said «she won't notice». What was wrong with the plan? | It only works if nobody notices, so it is a bad plan | It was fine, because mums never check their bank | It was risky only on weekends | It was wrong only for big amounts
exercise: What did Mia see on the bank statement? | Page after page of tiny game charges | One big charge for a brand new games console | Nothing, the list was empty | Only charges for food
exercise: How does Leo's family rule work? | One buy a month, and his mum types the password | He can buy whatever he wants, but only on Fridays | He uses her card when she is out | He buys only what his friends buy
exercise: Why was Jayden's mum calmer? | He told her before she found it herself | The purchase was very cheap, only a few pence | She did not care about money or what it was spent on | He hid it so well that she never knew about it at all
exercise: How did Zara make it right? | She paid it back from her pocket money over six weeks | She bought her dad a skin | She said sorry to her dad and then spent a bit more the next day | She asked her gran to pay it back for her
exercise: What did Kai's mum mean by «it's about the asking»? | Asking is about trust, whatever the price | Two pounds is too little to matter to anyone | Only big amounts need asking | Asking is only for birthdays
exercise: What is a wise setting for a family tablet? | A password needed for every purchase | A saved card with no password, to keep it quick | Shopping turned on for everyone | The card numbers written on the back
exercise: Ollie's sister wants to try the «free» chest in a game. What is wise? | Ask a parent first, because it may cost real money | Press it, free things in games never cost real money | Press it twice to be sure | Press it when nobody is watching
exercise: Which is a kind of courage in this lesson? | Telling a parent about a purchase before they find it | Hiding the bank statement under the sofa cushions for a month | Blaming your little brother | Deleting the purchase emails
test: «just use ur mum's card, she won't even notice». What do u say? | No, that's taking her money and she'll see it | Fine, but only for a small skin | OK, if you'll share the skin with me after | Sure, mums never read statements
test: Ollie: «it's game coins, not real money, innit». right? | No, the coins were bought with his mum's real money | Yes, game coins are always free to buy | Yes, as long as the coins stay inside the game and never leave it | Only real if they're gold coins
test: Ruby leaves the tablet with Rory, 3, for five minutes. What's the risk? | He may buy things by pressing buttons at random | He cannot press anything, he is far too small to reach | Tablets switch off near toddlers | Only the volume may change
test: Jayden bought a skin without asking. Wisest next move? | Tell his mum himself before she finds it | Wait and hope she never checks | Blame the game for a glitch that bought it | Delete the game so there's no proof
test: Kai: «it was only 2 quid, why's she so mad?» Best answer? | It's about not asking, not about the two pounds | Two pounds is a fortune | She's mad at the game, not him | She'd be fine if it were ten pounds
test: Priya finds a scrap with her dad's card numbers in her drawer. Best thing to do? | Throw it away and tell her dad | Keep it safe for emergencies only | Give it to a friend to hold for her | Use it just once and then bin it
test: Zara's dad: «you'll pay it back from your pocket money». Zara: «harsh!» What's true? | Paying back is a fair way to make it right | It is unfair because it was only a mistake | Children should never pay anything back | Only grown-ups pay money back
test: Mia's brother: «they're tiny charges, nobody'll see». What did the statement show? | Every tiny charge, page after page | Only the big charges | Nothing under five pounds | Just the total, no list
test: Leo's mum types the password for his one monthly buy. Why does this work so well? | The rule is clear and the purchase is always asked for | Leo can buy as much as he likes, as long as it's under a pound | His mum never checks what he buys | Leo knows the password anyway
test: mum, after a £70 surprise: «what do we change so this can't happen again?» Best idea? | Remove the saved card and add a purchase password | Hide the tablet behind the sofa | Ban the whole family from games for ever and sell the tablet | Buy a new tablet with no lock
test: a friend: «my mum got a refund last time, so it's fine to do it». What's true? | Refunds are not guaranteed, and it's still not asking | Refunds always happen, so it's fine | Shops pay you extra for mistakes | Asking is only needed without refunds
exam: Identify the principal reason for which currency acquired within a game ought not to be regarded as pretend money. | It is purchased with real funds from a real account | It may be exchanged solely within the game | It ceases to exist upon the closure of the game | It is supplied without charge to minors
exam: Whereas a minor has made a purchase without permission, select the response most consistent with established guidance. | The minor ought to disclose the purchase to a parent before its discovery | The minor ought to conceal the purchase and await events with patience | The minor ought to attribute the purchase to a sibling | The minor ought to make further purchases of equal value
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the use of a parent's payment card without consent? | It constitutes the taking of that parent's money | It is permissible where the sum is small | It is permissible where the card is saved on the device | It is permissible where a refund is likely
exam: Select the measure most consistent with the established facts for preventing unintended purchases by young children on a household device. | Requiring a password or lock for every purchase | Retaining the payment card on the device without restriction | Permitting purchases solely during daylight hours | Recording the card numbers upon the device
